=== exit status after a pipe
exit 2
--- stderr
[bendit] post-edit review:
  s.sh:1  Exit status after a pipe belongs to the LAST command, so this reports success even when the piped-from command failed. Use `set -o pipefail`, check PIPESTATUS/pipestatus, or don't pipe the thing you are testing.

A check that can pass for the wrong reason is worse than no check.

=== [ test ] && VAR under set -e
exit 2
--- stderr
[bendit] post-edit review:
  s.sh:2  `[ test ] && VAR=x` returns non-zero when the test is false. Under `set -e` that exits the script silently, mid-way, often with no output. Use if/then/fi.

A check that can pass for the wrong reason is worse than no check.

=== timeout (absent on macOS)
exit 2
--- stderr
[bendit] post-edit review:
  s.sh:1  `timeout` is GNU coreutils and is absent on macOS: the command never runs and whatever you assert afterwards passes unchanged. Use gtimeout, or a bounded retry loop.

A check that can pass for the wrong reason is worse than no check.

=== quote idiom in double quotes
exit 2
--- stderr
[bendit] post-edit review:
  s.sh:1  The '"'"' idiom escapes a quote inside a SINGLE-quoted string. Inside the double-quoted -c argument a single quote is already literal, so this injects garbage into the command.

A check that can pass for the wrong reason is worse than no check.

